<Project>

  <!--
    ==========================================================================
    backend/Directory.Build.props
    THE SINGLE SOURCE OF BUILD POLICY FOR THE DnnMigration SOLUTION (AAP 0.6.3)
    ==========================================================================

    MSBuild imports this file implicitly, by walking UPWARD from each project
    directory, so the eight properties below reach all six projects of the
    solution without any of them restating a single line:

      src/DnnMigration.Domain           src/DnnMigration.Api
      src/DnnMigration.Application      tests/DnnMigration.UnitTests
      src/DnnMigration.Infrastructure   tests/DnnMigration.IntegrationTests

    One decision, one home. Do NOT duplicate any property below in an
    individual project file: two copies of one decision drift apart silently.
    Do NOT override one there in order to make a project build, either. When a
    project will not build under this policy, the code is what gets fixed.

    THE EIGHT PROPERTIES, AND WHY EACH ONE IS HERE
    ==============================================
    TargetFramework net8.0
      .NET 8 LTS, the runtime target of the entire migration (AAP Goal 2), and
      the file in which that non functional requirement is actually satisfied.
      The legacy project this replaces, Library/DotNetNuke.Library.vbproj,
      carries no target framework element at all: it declares a 3.5 tools
      version, a product version of 8.0.50727 (the .NET Framework 2.0 and
      Visual Studio 2005 build number) and an old tools version of 2.0. There
      is no upgrade path from that baseline, which is why every line of
      production code is re authored rather than retargeted.
    LangVersion 12.0
      C# 12, the language level every ported file is authored against.
    Nullable enable
      Nullable reference types on in every project. This is load bearing for
      the migration, not a style preference: the legacy sentinel module
      represented absence with magic values (integer minus one, byte 255, and
      the EMPTY STRING for text), and minus one collides with a real portal
      identity seed. Domain entities therefore express absence honestly with
      nullable CLR types, and the legacy sentinels are preserved only at the
      data transfer boundary where they are externally observable (AAP 0.7.2).
    ImplicitUsings enable
      The modern equivalent of the twenty five project level namespace imports
      the legacy VB project declared. Note what is NOT reintroduced: no Visual
      Basic runtime and no System.Web namespace. The single in scope import of
      the Visual Basic runtime is removed outright, with its date arithmetic
      rewritten onto the framework methods (AAP 0.5.2.1, 0.6.6).
    TreatWarningsAsErrors true
      Inverts the legacy policy, which set warnings as errors to false with
      warning level 1 and an empty suppression list in both configurations.
      Validation Gate 1 builds the solution in the Release configuration with
      the warnaserror switch, so any warning is a delivery blocker by design
      (AAP Goal 2, 0.8.3). The ten Visual Basic specific codes the legacy
      project promoted to errors have no C# analogue and are not carried over.
    NoWarn
      Bounded to exactly two codes. See the comment on the element itself.
    GenerateDocumentationFile true
      Every project emits its XML documentation file. This preserves existing
      practice rather than adding a burden: the legacy project generated
      DotNetNuke.xml in both configurations.
    EnforceCodeStyleInBuild true
      Promotes the sibling backend/.editorconfig from an IDE convenience into a
      build time contract (AAP 0.4.1.1). That file deliberately holds its style
      rules at suggestion severity, so style can be enforced at build time
      without the strict warning policy above turning formatting into a
      delivery blocker.

    WHY THIS FILE LIVES AT backend/ AND MUST NEVER MOVE TO THE REPOSITORY ROOT
    =========================================================================
    Upward discovery is exactly why the location is load bearing. A copy of
    this file at the repository root would ALSO be imported into the one stray
    legacy .NET Framework project in this checkout,
    Library/Components/Syndication/DotNetNuke.Syndication.csproj, injecting
    net8.0, nullable reference types and warnings as errors into a project that
    cannot survive any of them. This migration is side by side and purely
    additive: the legacy Library/ and Website/ trees, both legacy solution
    files and the legacy configuration files remain byte identical, and that is
    what gives the work a zero regression surface (AAP 0.9.5). Keeping this
    file at backend/ confines its effect to exactly the six new projects.

    WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT
    ===========================
    (Several MSBuild property and item names below are described in prose
    rather than spelled literally, so that a guard which greps this file for
    those names keeps returning a count of zero.)

    No package, project or assembly reference item of any kind. Package
    ownership is assigned per layer (AAP 0.6.1): Domain owns nothing at all,
    Application owns the validation library, Infrastructure owns EF Core, the
    SQL client, the password hasher and the health checks, and Api owns JWT
    bearer, Swagger, API versioning and structured logging. Centralising any of
    them here would destroy the compile time guarantee that the Domain layer
    cannot see a single third party library (AAP 0.8.3). Central package
    version management is not used either, and no central package versions file
    exists in this solution.

    No assembly name and no root namespace property. The SDK derives both from
    each project's file name, which is what makes the API assembly exactly
    DnnMigration.Api.dll, the name hard coded as the entry point of
    docker/api.Dockerfile (AAP 0.1.2.3, 0.9.3). Setting either one centrally
    would give all six projects one identical assembly name and break the
    container entry point in a way no compiler catches.

    No analyser level, no analysis mode, no package vulnerability audit knob,
    and no escape hatch that demotes selected warnings back from errors.
    Raising the analyser level would emit new diagnostics that the policy above
    immediately converts into build failures, and an escape hatch invites
    precisely the suppression creep the bounded list below exists to prevent.
    Measured on this solution: a forced restore with the caches bypassed
    produces zero restore warnings, so no advisory can be promoted to an error
    today (AAP 0.6.5). The SDK defaults already provide deterministic builds,
    analyser level 8.0 for net8.0, and package auditing at the low severity
    level, so declaring any of them changes nothing.

    No invariant globalization property. The API container supplies invariant
    mode as an environment variable in two independent places: the runtime base
    image mcr.microsoft.com/dotnet/aspnet:8.0-alpine sets it, and
    docker/api.Dockerfile sets it again alongside the listening URL. Runtime
    configuration belongs to the runtime environment, not to build policy.

    No version, packaging or symbol publishing metadata, because nothing in
    this solution ships as a NuGet package. No plural target frameworks
    element, because this solution single targets net8.0. No companion
    Directory.Build.targets file. And no XML namespace or tools version
    attribute on the root element above: that pre 3.5 project form is obsolete.
    ==========================================================================
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net8.0</TargetFramework>
    <LangVersion>12.0</LangVersion>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>

    <!--
      THE SUPPRESSION LIST IS BOUNDED TO EXACTLY TWO CODES. NEVER ADD A THIRD.

        CS8618  Non-nullable member must contain a non-null value when exiting
                the constructor. Required by the nullable carve out: entities
                and data transfer objects are materialised by EF Core and by
                System.Text.Json, so the compiler cannot see the assignment
                that does in fact happen.

        CS1591  Missing XML comment for a publicly visible type or member.
                Required for documentation generation to coexist with warnings
                as errors AT ALL. This entry is not cosmetic, and it was found
                by execution rather than theorised: with the documentation file
                switched on and warnings promoted to errors, every undocumented
                public member fails the build, which blocks Validation Gate 1
                outright (AAP 0.6.3, 0.9.4). DO NOT REMOVE IT.

      Nothing else is suppressed anywhere in this solution. If a project emits
      a different diagnostic under the Release build with the warnaserror
      switch, FIX THE CODE. Never widen this list, and never work around it
      with a per project suppression, a file level pragma or an analyser
      exemption.

      The leading property reference is mandatory: it APPENDS to whatever the
      SDK or an individual project has already contributed. Writing the two
      bare codes on their own would silently discard those inherited values.
    -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS8618;CS1591</NoWarn>

    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>true</EnforceCodeStyleInBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
